- `bth repair --from-warm`: standalone command that reads all rows from `bathos.db`, projects down to the 13-field cool schema, and writes one Parquet file per `run_id` into a quarantine dir (`~/.bth/catalog/repair_staging/`) before atomically moving files to `cool/` only if no existing fragment is present (safe default; `--overwrite` flag to replace)

- Selective re-export via `bth repair --run-id <uuid>` or `--project <slug>` flags that filter the DuckDB query before re-emitting fragments, allowing targeted recovery without touching the full cool tier

- Schema-downcast registry in `schema.py`: a named tuple or dict mapping each cool-schema field to its warm-schema equivalent (some fields may be stored in the `metadata` JSON blob and need extraction), making the warm→cool projection explicit, testable, and version-aware rather than inlined in repair logic

- Export-to-quarantine-only mode (`bth repair --dry-run`): writes reconstructed fragments to `repair_staging/` and prints a diff summary (which run_ids would be created/replaced, field-level mismatches between reconstructed and existing) without touching `cool/`; lets the user audit before committing

- Integrate re-export as a `bth verify --repair` flag on the existing `verify.py` integrity checker: when `--tier cool` detects a missing or corrupted fragment, automatically attempt warm-sourced reconstruction in-place, logging each repair action to a `repair.jsonl` sidecar in the catalog dir

- Round-trip fidelity test harness in the test suite: after any `bth run` + `bth compact`, assert that re-exporting the warm row reproduces a byte-identical (or field-identical with explicit lossy-field exceptions) cool fragment, catching schema drift at CI time rather than at recovery time
